  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}^{200}$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 200$ matrices over $R$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\u2026,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\u2026,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R)$ and ${R}^{200}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200}(R))={200}^{2}=40\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{200})=200\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
